<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carlo I Tocco</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1429">1429</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tocco-family">Tocco family</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carlo I Tocco was Count palatine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kefalonia">Cephalonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakynthos">Zakynthos</a> from about 1376 and Duke of Leucadia. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a> in 1411 and Arta in 1416, reuniting the Despotate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> under his rule, and received the title of despot from the Byzantine emperor. After his death the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took Ioannina in 1430.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Carlo was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-i-tocco">Leonardo I Tocco</a>, count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kefalonia">Cephalonia</a> and duke of Leukas, and Maddalena de' Buondelmonti, sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esau-de-buondelmonti">Esau de' Buondelmonti</a>, the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a>. His grandmother Margherita Orsini was a sister of Nicholas Orsini and John II Orsini, rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, so he inherited a claim to Epirus from both families. He succeeded his father in 1376 and shared power with his brother Leonardo II, who received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakynthos">Zante</a> as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> in 1399. His marriage to Francesca, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nerio-i-acciaioli">Nerio I Acciaioli</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, gave him a claim to Corinth and Megara, which he seized in 1395.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His islands could not raise a large army, so he hired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a>, rewarding their leaders with gifts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a>. From 1405 he held fortresses on the mainland, including Angelokastro, and in February 1411 the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a> invited him to succeed his uncle Esau. The Albanian clans under Yaqub Spata of Arta defeated him in 1412 but failed to take the city; after Yaqub was killed in 1416, Arta surrendered, and the Tocchi held every major town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>. In 1415 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-ii-palaiologos">Manuel II Palaiologos</a> granted him the title despotes, and he signed his letters and decrees in Greek.</p><p>In 1421 he bought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glarentza">Glarentza</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> from Oliverio Franco. A raid on the animals of Albanian herders in late 1426 brought war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, and in 1427 John VIII Palaiologos's fleet defeated his at the Battle of the Echinades. In the settlement his niece Creusa married Constantine Palaiologos, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos">Constantine XI</a>, who received Glarentza as her dowry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Carlo died on 4 July 1429. He had no children with Francesca but five illegitimate sons and two daughters, one of whom married the Ottoman prince Musa Çelebi in 1412. He was succeeded by his nephew Carlo II Tocco, son of Leonardo II. The Chronicle of the Tocco, written by a contemporary and covering 1375 to 1425, is a main source for his rule.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carlo I Tocco” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1428 Constantine Palaiologos, the future last Byzantine emperor, gained Glarentza by marrying a niece of <b>Carlo I Tocco</b>, and in 1429–1430 he took Patras from its Latin archbishop.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1357 the county passed to Leonardo I Tocco, and his son <b>Carlo I Tocco</b> extended Tocco power to Ioannina and Arta.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-palatine-of-cephalonia-and-zakynthos">County Palatine of Cephalonia and Zakynthos</a></li><li><blockquote>After Gjin's death in 1399 his family held on briefly: Lepanto was sold to Venice in 1407, and Angelokastron fell to <b>Carlo I Tocco</b> around 1408.</blockquote><a href="/state/despotate-of-angelokastron-and-lepanto">Despotate of Angelokastron and Lepanto</a></li><li><blockquote>Yaqub Shpata was killed in 1416, and <b>Carlo I Tocco</b>, ruler of Ioannina, captured Arta.</blockquote><a href="/state/despotate-of-arta">Despotate of Arta</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Carlo I Tocco</b> reunited Ioannina and Arta from 1411, but the Ottomans took Ioannina in 1430, Arta in 1449 and the last fortresses in 1479.</blockquote><a href="/state/despotate-of-epirus">Despotate of Epirus</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kefalonia">Cephalonia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.27,20.55&amp;year=1419" aria-label="Cephalonia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5061 789 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Carlo I Tocco"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5511 1059h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5511 1059h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.27,20.55&amp;year=1419">What was here in 1419? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
